## an8.86
an8.86:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was wandering in the land of the Kosalans together with a large Saṅgha of mendicants when he arrived at a village of the Kosalan brahmins named Icchānaṅgala. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One, on a wandering tour among the Kosalans with a large Saṅgha of monks, arrived at a Kosalan brahman village named Icchānaṅgala.
an8.86:1.2 | S: He stayed in a forest near Icchānaṅgala. | T: There he stayed in the Icchānaṅgala forest grove.
an8.86:1.3 | S: The brahmins and householders of Icchānaṅgala heard: | T: The brahman householders of Icchānaṅgala heard it said,
an8.86:1.4 | S: “It seems the ascetic Gotama—a Sakyan, gone forth from a Sakyan family—has arrived at Icchānaṅgala. He is staying in a forest near Icchānaṅgala. | T: “Gotama the contemplative—the son of the Sakyans, having gone forth from the Sakyan clan—on a wandering tour among the Kosalans with a large Saṅgha of monks—has arrived at Icchānaṅgala and is staying in the Icchānaṅgala forest grove.
an8.86:1.5 | S: He has this good reputation: | T: And of that Master Gotama this fine reputation has spread:
an8.86:1.6 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ … | T: ‘He is indeed a Blessed One, worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed. He makes known—having realized it through direct knowledge—this world with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk; he explains the Dhamma admirable in the beginning, admirable in the middle, admirable in the end; he expounds the holy life both in its particulars & in its essence, entirely perfect, surpassingly pure.
an8.86:1.7 | S: It’s good to see such perfected ones.” | T: It is good to see such a worthy one.’”
an8.86:2.1 | S: Then, when the night had passed, they took abundant fresh and cooked foods and went to the forest near Icchānaṅgala, where they stood outside the gates making a colossal racket. | T: So the brahman householders of Icchānaṅgala, when the night was gone, taking many staple & non-staple foods, went to the gate house of the Icchānaṅgala forest grove. On arrival they stood there making a loud racket, a great racket.
an8.86:2.2 | S: Now, at that time Venerable Nāgita was the Buddha’s attendant. | T: Now at that time Ven. Nāgita was the Blessed One’s attendant.
an8.86:2.3 | S: Then the Buddha said to Nāgita, | T: So the Blessed One addressed Ven. Nāgita:
an8.86:2.4 | S: “Nāgita, who’s making that colossal racket? You’d think it was fishermen hauling in a catch!” | T: “Nāgita, what is that loud racket, that great racket, like fishermen with a catch of fish?”
an8.86:2.5 | S: “Sir, it’s these brahmins and householders of Icchānaṅgala. They’ve brought abundant fresh and cooked foods, and they’re standing outside the gates wanting to offer it specially to the Buddha and the mendicant Saṅgha.” | T: “Lord, those are the brahman householders of Icchānaṅgala standing at the gate house to the Icchānaṅgala forest grove, having brought many staple & non-staple foods for the sake of the Blessed One & the Saṅgha of monks.”
an8.86:2.6 | S: “Nāgita, may I never become famous. May fame not come to me. | T: “May I have nothing to do with honor, Nāgita, and honor nothing to do with me.
an8.86:2.7 | S: There are those who can’t get the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of awakening when they want, without trouble or difficulty like I can. | T: Whoever cannot obtain at will—without difficulty, without trouble—as I do, the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of self-awakening,
an8.86:2.8 | S: Let them enjoy the filthy, lazy pleasure of possessions, honor, and popularity.” | T: let him consent to this slimy-excrement-pleasure, this torpor-pleasure, this pleasure of gains, offerings, & fame.”
an8.86:3.1 | S: “Sir, may the Blessed One please relent now! May the Holy One relent! | T: “Lord, let the Blessed One acquiesce (to their offerings) now! Let the One Well-Gone acquiesce now!
an8.86:3.2 | S: Now is the time for the Buddha to relent. | T: Now is the time for the Blessed One’s acquiescence, lord! Now is the time for the Blessed One’s acquiescence, lord!
an8.86:3.3 | S: Wherever the Buddha now goes, the brahmins and householders, and people of town and country will incline the same way. | T: Wherever the Blessed One will go now, the brahmans of the towns & countryside will be so inclined.
an8.86:3.4 | S: It’s like when the heavens rain heavily and the water flows downhill. | T: Just as when the rain-devas send rain in fat drops, the waters flow with the incline,
an8.86:3.5 | S: In the same way, wherever the Buddha now goes, the brahmins and householders, and people of town and country will incline the same way. | T: in the same way, wherever the Blessed One will go now, the brahmans of the towns & countryside will be so inclined.
an8.86:3.6 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an8.86:3.7 | S: Because of the Buddha’s ethics and wisdom.” | T: Because such is the Blessed One’s virtue & discernment.”
an8.86:4.1 | S: “Nāgita, may I never become famous. May fame not come to me. | T: “May I have nothing to do with honor, Nāgita, and honor nothing to do with me.
an8.86:4.2 | S: There are those who can’t get the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of awakening when they want, without trouble or difficulty like I can. | T: Whoever cannot obtain at will—without difficulty, without trouble—as I do, the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of self-awakening,
an8.86:4.3 | S: Let them enjoy the filthy, lazy pleasure of possessions, honor, and popularity. | T: let him consent to this slimy-excrement-pleasure, this torpor-pleasure, this pleasure of gains, offerings, & fame.
an8.86:5.1 | S: Even some of the deities can’t get the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of awakening when they want, without trouble or difficulty like I can. | T: “Even some devas, Nāgita, cannot obtain at will—without difficulty, without trouble—as I do, the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of self-awakening.
an8.86:5.2 | S: When you all come together to enjoy each other’s company, I think: | T: When you all live together, assemble together, and live committed to dwelling with a group, the thought occurs:
an8.86:5.3 | S: ‘These venerables mustn’t get the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of awakening when they want, without trouble or difficulty like I can. | T: ‘Surely these venerable ones cannot obtain at will—without difficulty, without trouble—as I do, the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of self-awakening,
an8.86:5.4 | S: That must be because they come together to enjoy each other’s company.’ | T: which is why they live together, assemble together, and live committed to dwelling with a group.’
an8.86:6.1 | S: Take mendicants I see poking each other with their fingers, giggling and playing together. | T: [1] “There is the case, Nāgita, where I see monks laughing out loud, sporting around, tickling one another with their fingers.
an8.86:6.2 | S: I think to myself: | T: The thought occurs to me,
an8.86:6.3 | S: ‘These venerables mustn’t get the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of awakening when they want, without trouble or difficulty like I can. | T: ‘Surely these venerable ones cannot obtain at will—without difficulty, without trouble—as I do, the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of self-awakening,
an8.86:6.4 | S: That must be because they’re poking each other with their fingers, giggling and playing together.’ | T: which is why they are laughing out loud, sporting around, tickling one another with their fingers.’
an8.86:7.1 | S: Take mendicants I see eat as much as they like until their bellies are full, then indulge in the pleasures of sleeping, lying down, and drowsing. | T: [2] “Then there is the case where I see monks—having eaten as much as they want, filling their bellies—live committed to the pleasure of lying down, the pleasure of sensory contacts, the pleasure of torpor.
an8.86:7.2 | S: I think to myself: | T: The thought occurs to me,
an8.86:7.3 | S: ‘These venerables mustn’t get the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of awakening when they want, without trouble or difficulty like I can. | T: ‘Surely these venerable ones cannot obtain at will—without difficulty, without trouble—as I do, the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of self-awakening,
an8.86:7.4 | S: That must be because they eat as much as they like until their bellies are full, then indulge in the pleasures of sleeping, lying down, and drowsing.’ | T: which is why they—having eaten as much as they want, filling their bellies—live committed to the pleasure of lying down, the pleasure of sensory contacts, the pleasure of torpor.
an8.86:8.1 | S: Take a mendicant living within a village who I see sitting immersed in samādhi. | T: [3] “Then there is the case where I see a monk sitting in concentration in a village dwelling.
an8.86:8.2 | S: I think to myself: | T: The thought occurs to me,
an8.86:8.3 | S: ‘Now a monastery worker, a novice, or a fellow practitioner will make this venerable fall from immersion.’ | T: ‘Soon a monastery attendant will disturb this venerable one in some way, or a novice will, and rouse him from his concentration.’
an8.86:8.4 | S: So I’m not pleased that that mendicant is living within a village. | T: And so I am not pleased with that monk’s village-dwelling.
an8.86:9.1 | S: Take a mendicant in the wilderness who I see sitting nodding in meditation. | T: [4] “But then there is the case where I see a monk sitting, nodding, in the wilderness.
an8.86:9.2 | S: I think to myself: | T: The thought occurs to me,
an8.86:9.3 | S: ‘Now this venerable, having dispelled that sleepiness and weariness, will focus just on the unified perception of wilderness.’ | T: ‘Soon this venerable one will dispel his drowsiness & fatigue and attend to the wilderness-perception, (his mind) unified.’
an8.86:9.4 | S: So I’m pleased that that mendicant is living in the wilderness. | T: And so I am pleased with that monk’s wilderness-dwelling.
an8.86:10.1 | S: Take a mendicant in the wilderness who I see sitting without being immersed in samādhi. | T: [5] “Then there is the case where I see a wilderness monk sitting unconcentrated in the wilderness.
an8.86:10.2 | S: I think to myself: | T: The thought occurs to me,
an8.86:10.3 | S: ‘Now if this venerable’s mind is not immersed in samādhi they will immerse it; or if it is immersed in samādhi, they will preserve it.’ | T: ‘Soon this venerable one will center his unconcentrated mind, or protect his concentrated mind.’
an8.86:10.4 | S: So I’m pleased that that mendicant is living in the wilderness. | T: And so I am pleased with that monk’s wilderness-dwelling.
an8.86:11.1 | S: Take a mendicant in the wilderness who I see sitting immersed in samādhi. | T: [6] “Then there is the case where I see a wilderness monk sitting in concentration in the wilderness.
an8.86:11.2 | S: I think to myself: | T: The thought occurs to me,
an8.86:11.3 | S: ‘Now this venerable will free the unfreed mind or preserve the freed mind.’ | T: ‘Soon this venerable one will release his unreleased mind, or protect his released mind.’
an8.86:11.4 | S: So I’m pleased that that mendicant is living in the wilderness. | T: And so I am pleased with that monk’s wilderness-dwelling.
an8.86:12.1 | S: Take a mendicant who I see living within a village receiving robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: [7] “Then there is the case where I see a village-dwelling monk who receives robes, alms food, shelter, & medicinal requisites for curing the sick.
an8.86:12.2 | S: Enjoying possessions, honor, and popularity they neglect retreat, and they neglect remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest. | T: Receiving, as he likes, those gains, offerings, & fame, he neglects seclusion, he neglects isolated forest & wilderness dwellings.
an8.86:12.3 | S: They come down to villages, towns, and capital cities and make their home there. | T: He makes his living by visiting villages, towns, & cities.
an8.86:12.4 | S: So I’m not pleased that that mendicant is living within a village. | T: And so I am not pleased with that monk’s village-dwelling.
an8.86:13.1 | S: Take a mendicant who I see in the wilderness receiving robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: [8] “Then there is the case where I see a wilderness monk who receives robes, alms food, shelter, & medicinal requisites for curing the sick.
an8.86:13.2 | S: Fending off possessions, honor, and popularity they don’t neglect retreat, and they don’t neglect remote lodgings in the wilderness and the forest. | T: Fending off those gains, offerings, & fame, he doesn’t neglect seclusion, doesn’t neglect isolated forest & wilderness dwellings.
an8.86:13.3 | S: So I’m pleased that that mendicant is living in the wilderness. | T: And so I am pleased with that monk’s wilderness-dwelling.
an8.86:14.1 | S: Nāgita, when I’m walking along a road and I don’t see anyone ahead or behind I feel relaxed, even if I need to urinate or defecate.” | T: “But when I am traveling along a road and see no one in front or behind me, at that time I have my ease, even when urinating & defecating.”
an8.86:14.2 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.9
an8.9:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, you could rightly call Nanda ‘Gentleman’, | T: “Monks, speaking rightly of Nanda, one could say, ‘He is a son of a good family.’
an8.9:1.2 | S: ‘strong’, | T: Speaking rightly of Nanda, one could say, ‘He is strong.’
an8.9:1.3 | S: ‘lovely’, | T: Speaking rightly of Nanda, one could say, ‘He is handsome.’
an8.9:1.4 | S: and ‘lustful’. | T: Speaking rightly of Nanda, one could say, ‘He is fiercely passionate.’
an8.9:1.5 | S: How could he live the full and pure spiritual life unless he guards the sense doors, eats in moderation, is dedicated to wakefulness, and has mindfulness and situational awareness? | T: If Nanda did not guard the doors of his senses, did not know moderation in eating, was not devoted to wakefulness, and was not endowed with mindfulness & alertness, how would he be able to follow the holy life, perfect & pure?
an8.9:1.6 | S: This is how Nanda guards the sense doors. | T: “This is Nanda’s guarding of the doors of his senses:
an8.9:1.7 | S: If he has to look to the east, he wholeheartedly concentrates before looking, thinking: | T: If he should look to the east, he looks focusing his entire awareness, (thinking,)
an8.9:1.8 | S: ‘When I look to the east, bad, unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure will not overwhelm me.’ | T: ‘As I am looking thus to the east, greed & distress, evil unskillful qualities, will not flow out.’
an8.9:1.9 | S: In this way he’s aware of the situation. | T: That’s how he is alert there.
an8.9:2.1 | S: If he has to look to the west … | T: If he should look to the west…
an8.9:2.2 | S: north … | T: the north…
an8.9:2.3 | S: south … | T: the south…
an8.9:2.4 | S: up … | T: above…
an8.9:2.5 | S: down … | T: below…
an8.9:2.6 | S: If he has to survey the intermediate directions, he wholeheartedly concentrates before looking, thinking: | T: to the intermediate directions, he looks focusing his entire awareness, (thinking,)
an8.9:2.7 | S: ‘When I survey the intermediate directions, bad, unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure will not overwhelm me.’ | T: ‘As I am looking thus to the intermediate directions, greed & distress, evil unskillful qualities, will not flow out.’
an8.9:2.8 | S: In this way he’s aware of the situation. | T: That’s how he is alert there.
an8.9:2.9 | S: This is how Nanda guards the sense doors. | T: This is Nanda’s guarding of the doors of his senses.
an8.9:3.1 | S: This is how Nanda eats in moderation. | T: “This is Nanda’s knowledge of moderation in eating:
an8.9:3.2 | S: Nanda reflects rationally on the food he eats: | T: Nanda takes his food reflecting appropriately,
an8.9:3.3 | S: ‘Not for fun, indulgence, adornment, or decoration, but only to sustain this body, to avoid harm, and to support spiritual practice. In this way, I shall put an end to old discomfort and not give rise to new discomfort, and I will have the means to keep going, blamelessness, and a comfortable abiding.’ | T: not playfully, nor for intoxication, nor for putting on bulk, nor for beautification, but simply for the survival & continuance of this body, for ending its afflictions, for the support of the holy life, (thinking,) ‘I will destroy old feelings (of hunger) & not create new feelings (from overeating). Thus I will maintain myself, be blameless, & live in comfort.’
an8.9:3.4 | S: This is how Nanda eats in moderation. | T: This is Nanda’s knowledge of moderation in eating.
an8.9:4.1 | S: This is how Nanda is committed to wakefulness. | T: “This is Nanda’s devotion to wakefulness:
an8.9:4.2 | S: Nanda practices walking and sitting meditation by day, purifying his mind from obstacles. | T: There is the case where Nanda during the day, sitting & pacing back & forth, cleanses his mind of any qualities that would hold the mind in check.
an8.9:4.3 | S: In the first watch of the night, he continues to practice walking and sitting meditation. | T: During the first watch of the night [dusk to 10 p.m.], sitting & pacing back & forth, he cleanses his mind of any qualities that would hold the mind in check.
an8.9:4.4 | S: In the middle watch, he lies down in the lion’s posture—on the right side, placing one foot on top of the other—mindful and aware, and focused on the time of getting up. | T: During the second watch of the night [10 p.m. to 2 a.m.], reclining on his right side, he takes up the lion’s posture, one foot placed on top of the other, mindful, alert, with his mind set on getting up [either as soon as he awakens or at a particular time].
an8.9:4.5 | S: In the last watch, he gets up and continues to practice walking and sitting meditation, purifying his mind from obstacles. | T: During the last watch of the night [2 a.m. to dawn], sitting & pacing back & forth, he cleanses his mind of any qualities that would hold the mind in check.
an8.9:4.6 | S: This is how Nanda is committed to wakefulness. | T: This is Nanda’s devotion to wakefulness.
an8.9:5.1 | S: This is how Nanda has mindfulness and situational awareness. | T: “This is Nanda’s being in mindfulness & alertness:
an8.9:5.2 | S: Nanda knows feelings as they arise, as they remain, and as they go away. | T: There is the case where feelings are known to Nanda as they arise, known as they persist, known as they subside.
an8.9:5.3 | S: He knows perceptions as they arise, as they remain, and as they go away. | T: Perceptions are known as they arise, known as they persist, known as they subside.
an8.9:5.4 | S: He knows thoughts as they arise, as they remain, and as they go away. | T: Thoughts are known as they arise, known as they persist, known as they subside.
an8.9:5.5 | S: This is how Nanda has mindfulness and situational awareness. | T: This is Nanda’s being in mindfulness & alertness.
an8.9:6.1 | S: How could Nanda live the full and pure spiritual life unless he guards the sense doors, eats in moderation, is dedicated to wakefulness, and has mindfulness and situational awareness?” | T: “Monks, if Nanda did not guard the doors of his senses, did not know moderation in eating, was not devoted to wakefulness, and was not endowed with mindfulness & alertness, how would he be able to follow the holy life, perfect & pure?”
an8.9:6.2 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.13
an9.13:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita went up to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Mahā Koṭṭhita went to Ven. Sāriputta and exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an9.13:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to Sāriputta: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Sāriputta,
an9.13:1.3 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, is the spiritual life led under the Buddha for this goal: ‘May deeds to be experienced in this life be experienced by me in lives to come’?” | T: “Is it the case, friend Sāriputta, that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, ‘May an action (whose result) is to be experienced in the here & now be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me in a future life?‘”
an9.13:1.4 | S: “Certainly not, reverend.” | T: “No, my friend.”
an9.13:2.1 | S: “Then is the spiritual life led under the Buddha for this goal: ‘May deeds to be experienced in lives to come be experienced by me in this life’?” | T: “Then is it the case, friend Sāriputta, that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, ‘May an action (whose result) is to be experienced in a future life be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me in the here & now?‘”
an9.13:2.2 | S: “Certainly not.” | T: “No, my friend.”
an9.13:3.1 | S: “Is the spiritual life led under the Buddha for this goal: ‘May deeds to be experienced as pleasant be experienced by me as painful’?” | T: “Then is it the case, friend Sāriputta, that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, ‘May an action (whose result) is to be experienced as pleasure be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me as pain?‘”
an9.13:3.2 | S: “Certainly not.” | T: “No, my friend.”
an9.13:4.1 | S: “Then is the spiritual life led under the Buddha for this goal: ‘May deeds to be experienced as painful be experienced by me as pleasant’?” | T: “Then is it the case, friend Sāriputta, that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, ‘May an action (whose result) is to be experienced as pain be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me as pleasure?‘”
an9.13:4.2 | S: “Certainly not.” | T: “No, my friend.”
an9.13:5.1 | S: “Is the spiritual life led under the Buddha for this goal: ‘May deeds to be experienced when ripe be experienced by me when unripe’?” | T: “Then is it the case, friend Sāriputta, that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, ‘May an action (whose result) is to be experienced when fully ripened be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me when not fully ripened?‘”
an9.13:5.2 | S: “Certainly not.” | T: “No, my friend.”
an9.13:6.1 | S: “Then is the spiritual life led under the Buddha for this goal: ‘May deeds to be experienced when unripe be experienced by me when ripe’?” | T: “Then is it the case, friend Sāriputta, that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, ‘May an action (whose result) is to be experienced when not fully ripened be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me when fully ripened?‘”
an9.13:6.2 | S: “Certainly not.” | T: “No, my friend.”
an9.13:7.1 | S: “Is the spiritual life led under the Buddha for this goal: ‘May deeds to be experienced a lot be experienced by me a little’?” | T: “Then is it the case, friend Sāriputta, that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, ‘May an action (whose result) is to be experienced as a great deal be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me as next to nothing?‘”
an9.13:7.2 | S: “Certainly not.” | T: “No, my friend.”
an9.13:8.1 | S: “Then is the spiritual life led under the Buddha for this goal: ‘May deeds to be experienced a little be experienced by me a lot’?” | T: “Then is it the case, friend Sāriputta, that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, ‘May an action (whose result) is to be experienced as next to nothing be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me as a great deal?‘”
an9.13:8.2 | S: “Certainly not.” | T: “No, my friend.”
an9.13:9.1 | S: “Is the spiritual life led under the Buddha for this goal: ‘May deeds to be experienced by me be not experienced’?” | T: “Then is it the case, friend Sāriputta, that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, ‘May an action (whose result) is to be experienced be turned into an action (whose result) is not to be experienced by me?‘”
an9.13:9.2 | S: “Certainly not.” | T: “No, my friend.”
an9.13:10.1 | S: “Then is the spiritual life led under the Buddha for this goal: ‘May deeds not to be experienced be experienced’?” | T: “Then is it the case, friend Sāriputta, that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, ‘May an action (whose result) is not to be experienced be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me?‘”
an9.13:10.2 | S: “Certainly not.” | T: “No, my friend.”
an9.13:11.1 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, when you were asked whether the spiritual life was lived under the Buddha so that deeds to be experienced in this life are experienced in lives to come, you said, ‘Certainly not’. | T: “Now, friend Sāriputta, when you are asked, ‘Is it the case that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose, “May an action (whose result) is to be experienced in the here & now be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me in a future life?”’ you say, ‘No, my friend.’
an9.13:12.1 | S: When you were asked whether the spiritual life was lived under the Buddha so that deeds to be experienced in lives to come are experienced in this life … | T: When asked, ‘Is it the case that the holy life is lived under the “Blessed One with the purpose, “May an action (whose result) is to be experienced in a future life be turned into an action (whose results) is to be experienced by me in the here & now” …
an9.13:13.1 | S: deeds to be experienced as pleasant are experienced as painful … | T: “May an action (whose result) is to be experienced as pleasure be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me as pain?”…
an9.13:14.1 | S: deeds to be experienced as painful are experienced as pleasant … | T: “May an action (whose result) is to be experienced as pain be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me as pleasure?”
an9.13:15.1 | S: deeds to be experienced when ripe are experienced when unripe … | T: .…“May an action (whose result) is to be experienced when fully ripened be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me when not fully ripened?” …
an9.13:16.1 | S: deeds to be experienced when unripe are experienced when ripe … | T: “May an action (whose result) is to be experienced when not fully ripened be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me when fully ripened?” …
an9.13:17.1 | S: deeds to be experienced a lot are experienced a little … | T: “May an action (whose result) is to be experienced as a great deal be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me as next to nothing?” …
an9.13:18.1 | S: deeds to be experienced a little are experienced a lot … | T: “May an action (whose result) is to be experienced as next to nothing be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me as a great deal?”
an9.13:19.1 | S: deeds to be experienced are not experienced … | T: … “May an action (whose result) is to be experienced be turned into an action (whose result) is not to be experienced by me?”
an9.13:20.1 | S: When you were asked whether the spiritual life was lived under the Buddha so that deeds not to be experienced are experienced, you said, ‘Certainly not.’ | T: .…“May an action (whose result) is not to be experienced be turned into an action (whose result) is to be experienced by me?”’ you say, ‘No, my friend.’
an9.13:20.2 | S: Then what exactly is the goal of leading the spiritual life under the Buddha?” | T: So, then, with what purpose is the holy life lived under the Blessed One?”
an9.13:21.1 | S: “Reverend, the spiritual life is lived under the Buddha to know, see, attain, realize, and comprehend that which is unknown, unseen, unattained, unrealized, and uncomprehended.” | T: “The holy life, my friend, is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose of knowing, seeing, attaining, realizing, & breaking through to what has been unknown, unseen, unattained, unrealized, and not broken through to.“
an9.13:21.2 | S: “But what is the unknown, unseen, unattained, unrealized, and uncomprehended?” | T: “And what, friend Sāriputta, is the unknown, unseen, unattained, unrealized and not broken-through-to that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose of knowing, seeing, attaining, realizing, & breaking through to?”
an9.13:21.3 | S: “‘This is suffering.’ … | T: “‘This is stress,’ my friend;
an9.13:21.4 | P: Tassa ñāṇāya dassanāya pattiyā sacchikiriyāya abhisamayāya bhagavati brahmacariyaṁ vussati. | T: (none)
an9.13:21.5 | S: ‘This is the origin of suffering.’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering.’ … | T: ‘This is the origination of stress,’ my friend; ‘This is the cessation of stress,’ my friend;
an9.13:21.6 | S: ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering.’ … | T: ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress,’ my friend,
an9.13:21.7 | P: Tassa ñāṇāya dassanāya pattiyā sacchikiriyāya abhisamayāya bhagavati brahmacariyaṁ vussati. | T: is the unknown, unseen, unattained, unrealized and not broken-through-to that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose of knowing, seeing, attaining, realizing, & breaking through to.
an9.13:21.8 | S: This is the unknown, unseen, unattained, unrealized, and uncomprehended. | T: This is the unknown, unseen, unattained, unrealized and not broken-through-to
an9.13:21.9 | S: The spiritual life is lived under the Buddha to know, see, attain, realize, and comprehend this.” | T: that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose of knowing, seeing, attaining, realizing, & breaking through to.”
an9.13:21.10 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.14
an9.14:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Samiddhi went up to Venerable Sāriputta, bowed, and sat to one side. Venerable Sāriputta said to him: | T: Then Ven. Samiddhi went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, Ven. Sāriputta said to him,
an9.14:2.1 | S: “Samiddhi, based on what do thoughts arise in a person?” | T: “Based on what, Samiddhi, do thoughts & resolves arise in a person?”
an9.14:2.2 | S: “Based on name and form, sir.” | T: “Based on name & form, sir.”
an9.14:3.1 | S: “Where do they become diversified?” | T: “And how do they go to multiplicity?”
an9.14:3.2 | S: “In the elements.” | T: “Through the properties, sir.”
an9.14:4.1 | S: “What is their origin?” | T: “And what do they have as their origination?”
an9.14:4.2 | S: “Contact is their origin.” | T: “They have contact as their origination, sir.”
an9.14:5.1 | S: “What is their meeting place?” | T: “And what do they have as their meeting place?”
an9.14:5.2 | S: “Feeling is their meeting place.” | T: “They have feeling as their meeting place, sir.”
an9.14:6.1 | S: “What is their chief?” | T: “And what do they have as their presiding state?”
an9.14:6.2 | S: “Immersion is their chief.” | T: “They have concentration as their presiding state, sir.”
an9.14:7.1 | S: “What is their ruler?” | T: “And what do they have as their governing principle?”
an9.14:7.2 | S: “Mindfulness is their ruler.” | T: “They have mindfulness as their governing principle, sir.”
an9.14:8.1 | S: “What is their overseer?” | T: “And what do they have as their surpassing state?”
an9.14:8.2 | S: “Wisdom is their overseer.” | T: “They have discernment as their surpassing state, sir.”
an9.14:9.1 | S: “What is their core?” | T: “And what do they have as their heartwood?”
an9.14:9.2 | S: “Freedom is their core.” | T: “They have release as their heartwood, sir.”
an9.14:10.1 | S: “What is their objective?” | T: “And where do they gain their footing?”
an9.14:10.2 | S: “Their objective is freedom from death.” | T: “They gain their footing in the deathless, sir.”
an9.14:11.1 | S: “Samiddhi, when you were asked what is the basis on which thoughts arise in a person, you answered ‘name and form’. | T: “Samiddhi, on being asked, ‘Based on what do thoughts & resolves arise in a person?’ you have answered, ‘Based on name & form.’
an9.14:11.2 | S: When you were asked … | T: “When asked, ‘And how do they go to multiplicity?’ you have answered, ‘Through the properties.’
an9.14:11.3 | P: ‘Te pana, samiddhi, kiṁsamudayā’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno ‘phassasamudayā, bhante’ti vadesi. | T: “When asked, ‘And what do they have as their origination?’ you have answered, ‘They have contact as their origination.’
an9.14:11.4 | P: ‘Te pana, samiddhi, kiṁsamosaraṇā’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno ‘vedanāsamosaraṇā, bhante’ti vadesi. | T: “When asked, ‘And what do they have as their meeting place?’ you have answered, ‘They have feeling as their meeting place.’
an9.14:11.5 | P: ‘Te pana, samiddhi, kiṁpamukhā’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno ‘samādhippamukhā, bhante’ti vadesi. | T: “When asked, ‘And what do they have as their presiding state?’ you have answered, ‘They have concentration as their presiding state.’
an9.14:11.6 | P: ‘Te pana, samiddhi, kiṁadhipateyyā’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno ‘satādhipateyyā, bhante’ti vadesi. | T: “When asked, ‘And what do they have as their governing principle?’ you have answered, ‘They have mindfulness as their governing principle.’
an9.14:11.7 | P: ‘Te pana, samiddhi, kiṁuttarā’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno ‘paññuttarā, bhante’ti vadesi. | T: “When asked, ‘And what do they have as their surpassing state?’ you have answered, ‘They have discernment as their surpassing state.’
an9.14:11.8 | P: ‘Te pana, samiddhi, kiṁsārā’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno ‘vimuttisārā, bhante’ti vadesi. | T: “When asked, ‘And what do they have as their heartwood?’ you have answered, ‘They have release as their heartwood.’
an9.14:11.9 | S: what is their objective, you answered ‘freedom from death’. | T: “When asked, ‘And where do they gain their footing?’ you have answered, ‘They gain their footing in the deathless.’
an9.14:11.10 | S: Good, good, Samiddhi! | T: “Very good, Samiddhi, very good.
an9.14:11.11 | S: It’s good that you answered each question. But don’t get conceited because of that.” | T: It’s good, the way you have answered when questioned, but don’t get conceited about that.”
an9.14:11.12 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.16

