 succeeded in 0ms:
an9.16:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these nine perceptions, when developed and cultivated, are very fruitful and beneficial. Their objective and culmination is freedom from death. | T: “Monks, these nine perceptions, when developed & pursued, are of great fruit, of great benefit. They gain a footing in the deathless and have the deathless as their final end.
an9.16:1.2 | S: What nine? | T: Which nine?
an9.16:1.3 | S: The perceptions of ugliness, death, repulsiveness of food, dissatisfaction with the whole world, impermanence, suffering in impermanence, not-self in suffering, giving up, and fading away. | T: “The perception of unattractiveness (of the body), the perception of death, the perception of loathsomeness in food, the perception of distaste for every world, the perception of inconstancy, the perception of stress in inconstancy, the perception of not-self in stress, the perception of abandoning, the perception of dispassion.
an9.16:1.4 | S: These nine perceptions, when developed and cultivated, are very fruitful and beneficial. Their objective and culmination is freedom from death.” | T: “These nine perceptions, when developed & pursued, are of great fruit, of great benefit. They gain a footing in the deathless and have the deathless as their final end.”
an9.16:1.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.1
an9.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
an9.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
an9.1:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There he said to the monks:
an9.1:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, if wanderers of other religions were to ask: | T: “Monks, if wanderers who are members of other sects should ask you,
an9.1:2.2 | S: ‘Reverends, what is the vital condition for the development of the qualities on the same side as awakening?’ How would you answer them?” | T: ‘What, friend, are the prerequisites for the development of the wings to self-awakening?’ how would you answer them?”
an9.1:2.3 | S: “Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. … The mendicants will listen and remember it.” | T: “For us, lord, the teachings have the Blessed One as their root, their guide, & their arbitrator. It would be good if the Blessed One himself would explicate the meaning of this statement. Having heard it from the Blessed One, the monks will remember it.”
an9.1:3.1 | S: “Well then, mendicants, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “In that case, monks, listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
an9.1:3.2 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to him.
an9.1:3.3 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
an9.1:4.1 | S: “Mendicants, if wanderers of other religions were to ask: | T: “If wanderers who are members of other sects should ask you,
an9.1:4.2 | S: ‘Reverends, what is the vital condition for the development of the qualities on the same side as awakening?’ You should answer them: | T: ‘What, friend, are the prerequisites for the development of the wings to self-awakening?’ you should answer,
an9.1:5.1 | S: ‘It’s when a mendicant has good friends, companions, and associates. | T: ‘There is the case where a monk has admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues.
an9.1:5.2 | S: This is the first vital condition for the development of the awakening factors. | T: This is the first prerequisite for the development of the wings to self-awakening.
an9.1:6.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: “‘And further, the monk is virtuous. He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
an9.1:6.2 | S: This is the second vital condition for the development of the qualities on the same side as awakening. | T: This is the second prerequisite for the development of the wings to self-awakening.
an9.1:7.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant gets to take part in talk about self-effacement that helps open the heart, when they want, without trouble or difficulty. That is, talk about fewness of wishes, contentment, seclusion, aloofness, arousing energy, ethics, immersion, wisdom, freedom, and the knowledge and vision of freedom. | T: “‘And further, he gets to hear at will, easily & without difficulty, talk that is truly sobering & conducive to the opening of awareness, i.e., talk on modesty, contentment, seclusion, non-entanglement, arousing persistence, virtue, concentration, discernment, release, and the knowledge & vision of release.
an9.1:7.2 | S: This is the third vital condition for the development of the qualities on the same side as awakening. | T: This is the third prerequisite for the development of the wings to self-awakening.
an9.1:8.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant lives with energy roused up for giving up unskillful qualities and embracing skillful qualities. They are strong, staunchly vigorous, not slacking off when it comes to developing skillful qualities. | T: “‘And further, he keeps his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful qualities and for taking on skillful qualities. He is steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful qualities.
an9.1:8.2 | S: This is the fourth vital condition for the development of the qualities on the same side as awakening. | T: This is the fourth prerequisite for the development of the wings to self-awakening.
an9.1:9.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is wise. They have the wisdom of arising and passing away which is noble, penetrative, and leads to the complete ending of suffering. | T: “‘And further, he is discerning, endowed with the discernment of arising & passing away—noble, penetrating, leading to the right ending of stress.
an9.1:9.2 | S: This is the fifth vital condition for the development of the qualities on the same side as awakening.’ | T: This is the fifth prerequisite for the development of the wings to self-awakening.’
an9.1:10.1 | S: A mendicant with good friends, companions, and associates can expect to | T: “Monks, when a monk has admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues, it is to be expected that he will
an9.1:10.2 | S: be ethical … | T: be virtuous, will dwell restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity, and will train himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
an9.1:11.1 | S: A mendicant with good friends, companions, and associates can expect to | T: “When a monk has admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues, it is to be expected that he will
an9.1:11.2 | S: take part in talk about self-effacement that helps open the heart … | T: get to hear at will, easily & without difficulty, talk that is truly sobering and conducive to the opening of awareness, i.e., talk on modesty, contentment, seclusion, non-entanglement, arousing persistence, virtue, concentration, discernment, release, and the knowledge & vision of release.
an9.1:12.1 | S: A mendicant with good friends, companions, and associates can expect to | T: “When a monk has admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues, it is to be expected that he will
an9.1:12.2 | S: live with energy roused up … | T: keep his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful qualities and for taking on skillful qualities—steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful qualities.
an9.1:13.1 | S: A mendicant with good friends, companions, and associates can expect to | T: “When a monk has admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues, it is to be expected that he will
an9.1:13.2 | S: be wise … | T: be discerning, endowed with discernment of arising & passing away—noble, penetrating, leading to the right ending of stress.
an9.1:14.1 | S: But then, a mendicant grounded on these five things should develop four further things. | T: “And further, monks, when the monk is established in these five qualities, there are four additional qualities he should develop:
an9.1:14.2 | S: They should develop the perception of ugliness to give up greed, love to give up hate, mindfulness of breathing to cut off thinking, and perception of impermanence to uproot the conceit ‘I am’. | T: He should develop (contemplation of) the unattractive so as to abandon lust. He should develop goodwill so as to abandon ill will. He should develop mindfulness of in-&-out breathing so as to cut off distractive thinking. He should develop the perception of inconstancy so as to uproot the conceit, ‘I am.’
an9.1:14.3 | S: When you perceive impermanence, the perception of not-self becomes stabilized. | T: For a monk perceiving inconstancy, the perception of not-self is made firm.
an9.1:14.4 | S: Perceiving not-self, you uproot the conceit ‘I am’ and attain extinguishment in this very life.” | T: One perceiving not-self attains the uprooting of the conceit, ‘I am’—unbinding in the here & now.”
an9.1:14.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.20
an9.20:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
an9.20:1.2 | S: Then the householder Anāthapiṇḍika went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him, | T: Then Anāthapiṇḍika the householder went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, bowed down to him and sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him,
an9.20:2.1 | S: “Householder, I wonder whether your family gives gifts?” | T: “Householder, are gifts still given in your family?”
an9.20:2.2 | S: “It does, sir. | T: “Gifts are still given in my family, lord,
an9.20:2.3 | S: But only coarse sour gruel with false black pepper.” | T: but they are coarse: broken rice cooked with bran, accompanied by pickle brine.”
an9.20:2.4 | S: “Householder, someone might give a gift that’s either coarse or fine. | T: “Householder, regardless of whether a gift is coarse or refined,
an9.20:2.5 | S: But they give it carelessly, thoughtlessly, not with their own hand. They give the dregs, and they give without consideration for consequences. | T: if it is given inattentively, disrespectfully, not with one’s own hand, as if throwing it away, with the view that nothing will come of it:
an9.20:2.6 | S: Then wherever the result of any such gift manifests, their mind doesn’t incline toward enjoyment of nice food, clothes, vehicles, or the five refined kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: Wherever the result of that gift comes to fruition, one’s mind will not incline to the enjoyment of splendid food, will not incline to the enjoyment of splendid clothing, will not incline to the enjoyment of splendid vehicles, will not incline to the enjoyment of the splendid five strings of sensuality.
an9.20:2.7 | S: And their children, wives, bondservants, servants, and workers don’t want to listen to them. They don’t actively listen or try to understand. | T: And one’s sons & daughters, slaves, servants, & workers will not listen to one, will not lend ear, will not make their minds attend for the sake of knowledge.
an9.20:2.8 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an9.20:2.9 | S: Because that is the result of deeds done carelessly. | T: Because that is the result of inattentive actions.
an9.20:3.1 | S: Someone might give a gift that’s either coarse or fine. | T: “Householder, regardless of whether a gift is coarse or refined,
an9.20:3.2 | S: And they give it carefully, thoughtfully, with their own hand. They don’t give the dregs, and they give with consideration for consequences. | T: if it is given attentively, respectfully, with one’s own hand, not as if throwing it away, with the view that something will come of it:
an9.20:3.3 | S: Then wherever the result of any such gift manifests, their mind inclines toward enjoyment of nice food, clothes, vehicles, or the five refined kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: Wherever the result of that gift comes to fruition, one’s mind will incline to the enjoyment of splendid food, will incline to the enjoyment of splendid clothing, will incline to the enjoyment of splendid vehicles, will incline to the enjoyment of the splendid five strings of sensuality.
an9.20:3.4 | S: And their children, wives, bondservants, servants, and workers want to listen. They actively listen and try to understand. | T: And one’s sons & daughters, slaves, servants, & workers will listen to one, will lend ear, will make their minds attend for the sake of knowledge.
an9.20:3.5 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an9.20:3.6 | S: Because that is the result of deeds done carefully. | T: Because that is the result of attentive actions.
an9.20:4.1 | S: Once upon a time, householder, there was a brahmin named Velāma. | T: “Once, householder, there was a brahman named Velāma.
an9.20:4.2 | S: He gave the following gift, a great offering. | T: And this was the nature of the gift, the great gift, he gave:
an9.20:4.3 | S: 84,000 gold cups filled with silver. 84,000 silver cups filled with gold. 84,000 bronze cups filled with gold coins. 84,000 elephants with gold adornments and banners, covered with snow gold netting. 84,000 chariots upholstered with the hide of lions, tigers, and leopards, and cream rugs, with gold adornments and banners, covered with snow gold netting. 84,000 milk cows with muslin halter and bronze pails. 84,000 maidens bedecked with jeweled earrings. 84,000 couches spread with woolen covers—shag-piled, pure white, or embroidered with flowers—and spread with a fine deer hide, with canopies above and red pillows at both ends. 8,400,000,000 cloths of delicate linen, cotton, silk, and wool. And who can say how much food, drink, snacks, meals, refreshments, and beverages? It seemed like an overflowing river. | T: He gave 84,000 gold trays filled with silver, 84,000 silver trays filled with gold, 84,000 copper trays filled with gems. He gave 84,000 elephants with gold ornaments, gold banners, covered with nets of gold thread. He gave 84,000 chariots spread with lion skins, tiger skins, leopard skins, saffron-colored blankets, with gold ornaments, gold banners, covered with nets of gold thread. He gave 84,000 milk cows with tethers of fine jute and copper milk pails. He gave 84,000 maidens adorned with jeweled earrings. He gave 84,000 couches spread with long-fleeced coverlets, white wool coverlets, embroidered coverlets, rugs of kadali-deer hide, each with a canopy above & red cushions on either side. He gave 84,000 lengths of cloth—of finest linen, of finest cotton, of finest silk. To say nothing of the food & drink, staple & non-staple food, lotions & beddings: They flowed, as it were, like rivers.
an9.20:5.1 | S: Householder, you might think: | T: “Now, householder, if the thought should occur to you,
an9.20:5.2 | S: ‘Surely the brahmin Velāma must have been someone else at that time?’ | T: ‘Perhaps it was someone else who at that time was Velāma the brahman, who gave that gift, that great gift,’
an9.20:5.3 | S: But you should not see it like this. | T: that’s not how it should be seen.
an9.20:5.4 | S: I myself was the brahmin Velāma at that time. | T: I was Velāma the brahman at that time.
an9.20:5.5 | S: I gave that gift, a great offering. | T: I gave that gift, that great gift.
an9.20:5.6 | S: But at that event there was no one worthy of a religious donation, and no one to purify the religious donation. | T: But in that gift there was no one worthy of offerings; no one purified that gift.
an9.20:6.1 | S: It would be more fruitful to feed one individual accomplished in view than that great offering of Velāma. | T: “If one were to feed one person consummate in view, that would be more fruitful than the gift, the great gift, that Velāma the brahman gave.
an9.20:7.1 | S: It would be more fruitful to feed one once-returner than a hundred of those accomplished in view. | T: “If one were to feed one once-returner, that would be more fruitful than the gift, the great gift, that Velāma the brahman gave, and if one were to feed one person consummate in view, and if one were to feed 100 people consummate in view.
an9.20:8.1 | S: It would be more fruitful to feed one non-returner than a hundred once-returners. | T: “If one were to feed one non-returner, that would be more fruitful than… if one were to feed 100 once-returners.
an9.20:8.2 | S: It would be more fruitful to feed one perfected one than a hundred non-returners. | T: “If one were to feed one arahant, that would be more fruitful than… if one were to feed 100 non-returners.
an9.20:8.3 | S: It would be more fruitful to feed one independent Buddha than a hundred perfected ones. | T: “If one were to feed one Private Buddha, that would be more fruitful than… if one were to feed 100 arahants.
an9.20:8.4 | S: It would be more fruitful to feed one Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha than a hundred independent Buddhas. | T: “If one were to feed one Tathāgata—a worthy one, rightly self-awakened—that would be more fruitful than… if one were to feed 100 Private Buddhas.
an9.20:8.5 | S: It would be more fruitful to feed the mendicant Saṅgha headed by the Buddha than to feed one Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha. | T: “If one were to feed a Saṅgha of monks headed by the Buddha, that would be more fruitful than… if one were to feed a Tathāgata—a worthy one, rightly self-awakened.
an9.20:8.6 | S: It would be more fruitful to build a dwelling especially for the Saṅgha of the four quarters than to feed the mendicant Saṅgha headed by the Buddha. | T: “If one were to have a dwelling built and dedicated to the Community of the four directions, that would be more fruitful than… if one were to feed a Saṅgha of monks headed by the Buddha.
an9.20:8.7 | S: It would be more fruitful to go for refuge to the Buddha, the teaching, and the Saṅgha with a confident heart than to build a dwelling for the Saṅgha of the four quarters. | T: “If one with a confident mind were to go to the Buddha, Dhamma, & Saṅgha for refuge, that would be more fruitful than… if one were to have a dwelling built and dedicated to the Community of the four directions.
an9.20:8.8 | S: It would be more fruitful to undertake the training rules—not to kill living creatures, steal, commit sexual misconduct, lie, or consume intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor—than to go for refuge to the Buddha, the teaching, and the Saṅgha with a confident heart. | T: “If one with a confident mind were to undertake the training rules—refraining from taking life, refraining from taking what is not given, refraining from sexual misconduct, refraining from lying, refraining from distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness—that would be more fruitful than… if one with a confident mind were to go to the Buddha, Dhamma, & Saṅgha for refuge.
an9.20:8.9 | S: It would be more fruitful to develop a heart of love—even just as long as it takes to pull a cow’s udder—than to undertake the training rules. | T: “If one were to develop even just one whiff of a heart of goodwill, that would be more fruitful than… if one with a confident mind were to undertake the training rules.…
an9.20:9.1 | S: It would be more fruitful to develop the perception of impermanence—even as long as a finger-snap—than to do all of these things, including developing a heart of love for as long as it takes to pull a cow’s udder.” | T: “If one were to develop even for just a finger-snap the perception of inconstancy, that would be more fruitful than the gift, the great gift, that Velāma the brahman gave, and if one were to feed one person…
an9.20:9.2 | P: yo ca sataṁ diṭṭhisampannānaṁ bhojeyya, yo cekaṁ sakadāgāmiṁ bhojeyya … | T: 100 people consummate in view, and if one were to feed one once-returner…
an9.20:9.3 | P: yo ca sataṁ sakadāgāmīnaṁ bhojeyya, yo cekaṁ anāgāmiṁ bhojeyya … | T: 100 once-returners, and if one were to feed one non-returner…
an9.20:9.4 | P: yo ca sataṁ anāgāmīnaṁ bhojeyya, yo cekaṁ arahantaṁ bhojeyya … | T: 100 non-returners, and if one were to feed one arahant…
an9.20:9.5 | P: yo ca sataṁ arahantānaṁ bhojeyya, yo cekaṁ paccekabuddhaṁ bhojeyya … | T: 100 arahants, and if one were to feed one Private Buddha…
an9.20:9.6 | P: yo ca sataṁ paccekabuddhānaṁ bhojeyya, yo ca tathāgataṁ arahantaṁ sammāsambuddhaṁ bhojeyya … | T: 100 Private Buddhas, and if one were to feed a Tathāgata—a worthy one, rightly self-awakened—
an9.20:9.7 | P: yo ca buddhappamukhaṁ bhikkhusaṅghaṁ bhojeyya, yo ca cātuddisaṁ saṅghaṁ uddissa vihāraṁ kārāpeyya … | T: and if one were to feed a Saṅgha of monks headed by the Buddha, and if one were to have a dwelling built and dedicated to the Community of the four directions,
an9.20:9.8 | P: yo ca pasannacitto buddhañca dhammañca saṅghañca saraṇaṁ gaccheyya, yo ca pasannacitto sikkhāpadāni samādiyeyya— | T: and if one with a confident mind were to go to the Buddha, Dhamma, & Saṅgha for refuge, and if one with a confident mind were to undertake the training rules—
an9.20:9.9 | P: pāṇātipātā veramaṇiṁ …pe… surāmerayamajjapamādaṭṭhānā veramaṇiṁ, yo ca antamaso gandhohanamattampi mettacittaṁ bhāveyya, yo ca accharāsaṅghātamattampi aniccasaññaṁ bhāveyya, idaṁ tato mahapphalataran”ti. | T: refraining from taking life, refraining from taking what is not given, refraining from sexual misconduct, refraining from lying, refraining from distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness—and if one were to develop even just one whiff of a heart of goodwill.”
an9.20:9.10 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an9.20:9.11 | P: Sīhanādavaggo dutiyo. | T: (none)
an9.20:10.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
an9.20:10.1 | P: Nādo saupādiseso ca, | T: (none)
an9.20:10.2 | P: koṭṭhikena samiddhinā; | T: (none)
an9.20:10.3 | P: Gaṇḍasaññā kulaṁ mettā, | T: (none)
an9.20:10.4 | P: devatā velāmena cāti. | T: (none)


